\f0\b \cf2 11. Which part of the plant absorbs water and mineral salts from the soil?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Stem\
\
(B) Leaf\
\
(C) Root\
\
(D) Flower\
\

\f0\b Answer: (C) Root.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Roots anchor the plant and absorb moisture and essential minerals from the soil.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 12. The process by which green leaves prepare food using sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide is called:
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Respiration\
\
(B) Photosynthesis\
\
(C) Transpiration\
\
(D) Germination\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Photosynthesis.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Chlorophyll in leaves traps sunlight to produce glucose through photosynthesis.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 13. Night blindness is caused by the deficiency of which vitamin?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Vitamin A\
\
(B) Vitamin B\
\
(C) Vitamin C\
\
(D) Vitamin D\
\

\f0\b Answer: (A) Vitamin A.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Lack of Vitamin A impairs night vision and damages eye health.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 14. Which nutrient acts as the primary "bodybuilder" to help children grow and repair injured tissues?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Carbohydrates\
\
(B) Fats\
\
(C) Proteins\
\
(D) Roughage\
\

\f0\b Answer: (C) Proteins.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Proteins are essential building blocks for muscle growth, tissue repair, and cell maintenance.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 15. Weak, soft, and bent bones in young children (Rickets) are caused by a lack of:
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Vitamin D\
\
(B) Vitamin C\
\
(C) Vitamin K\
\
(D) Iron\
\

\f0\b Answer: (A) Vitamin D.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Vitamin D and calcium are required for bone hardening; a deficiency results in rickets.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 16. Animals that eat only plants or plant parts are called:
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Carnivores\
\
(B) Herbivores\
\
(C) Omnivores\
\
(D) Scavengers\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Herbivores.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Herbivores (like cows, deer, and rabbits) feed strictly on plant matter.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 17. Which of the following animals is an omnivore?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Cow\
\
(B) Tiger\
\
(C) Crow\
\
(D) Lion\
\

\f0\b Answer: (C) Crow.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Crows eat both grains/seeds and small insects or meat scraps.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 18. The loss of excess water as water vapor from leaf stomata into the air is known as:
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Evaporation\
\
(B) Condensation\
\
(C) Transpiration\
\
(D) Precipitation\
\

\f0\b Answer: (C) Transpiration.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Transpiration cools the plant and creates suction to pull water upward from the roots.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 19. Which type of root system does a grass plant have?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Taproot system\
\
(B) Fibrous root system\
\
(C) Stilt root system\
\
(D) Aerial root system\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Fibrous root system.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Grasses have a bunch of similar, thin roots originating together, typical of monocot plants.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 20. The elbow and knee joints allow bending movement in only one direction. They are examples of:
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Ball and socket joints\
\
(B) Hinge joints\
\
(C) Pivotal joints\
\
(D) Fixed joints\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Hinge joints.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Like the hinge of a door, a hinge joint moves strictly backward and forward in one plane.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 21. Fish breathe underwater using which specialized organ?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Lungs\
\
(B) Gills\
\
(C) Skin\
\
(D) Fins\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Gills.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Gills absorb dissolved oxygen from the water as it passes through them.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 22. Which adaptation helps camels survive long periods without water in hot deserts?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Thick fur all over their eyes\
\
(B) Storing fat in their hump and passing very little urine\
\
(C) Webbed feet for paddling\
\
(D) Moist skin for breathing\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Storing fat in their hump and passing very little urine.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  These features conserve moisture and provide metabolic energy over long dry spells.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 23. An object that does NOT allow any light to pass through it is described as:
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Transparent\
\
(B) Translucent\
\
(C) Opaque\
\
(D) Luminous\
\

\f0\b Answer: (C) Opaque.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Opaque objects (like wood, brick, or cardboard) block light completely, forming shadows.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 24. Which of the following is a natural source of light?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Torch bulb\
\
(B) Tube light\
\
(C) The Sun\
\
(D) Burning candle\
\

\f0\b Answer: (C) The Sun.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  The Sun produces its own natural light, while torches and candles are artificial sources.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 25. What is the standard SI unit of length?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Centimeter\
\
(B) Meter\
\
(C) Kilometer\
\
(D) Millimeter\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Meter.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Under the International System of Units (SI), the meter (m) is the standard base unit for length.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 26. The motion of a stone dropped from the top of a terrace straight down to the ground is:
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Circular motion\
\
(B) Rectilinear (straight-line) motion\
\
(C) Periodic motion\
\
(D) Rotational motion\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Rectilinear (straight-line) motion.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Motion along a straight track or vertical drop is rectilinear.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 27. The back-and-forth movement of a swing repeats at regular intervals. This motion is:
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Rectilinear motion\
\
(B) Periodic motion\
\
(C) Random motion\
\
(D) Circular motion\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Periodic motion.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Motion that repeats itself after regular intervals of time is periodic.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 28. An electric cell converts which form of energy into electrical energy?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Heat energy\
\
(B) Chemical energy\
\
(C) Light energy\
\
(D) Sound energy\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Chemical energy.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Chemicals stored inside the battery react to produce an electric current.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 29. Which of the following is a good electrical conductor?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Plastic comb\
\
(B) Wooden toothpick\
\
(C) Iron nail\
\
(D) Rubber band\
\

\f0\b Answer: (C) Iron nail.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Metals like iron allow electric current to flow through them easily.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 30. Why are electric wires covered with plastic sheaths?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Plastic makes wires look attractive\
\
(B) Plastic is an insulator that protects us from electric shocks\
\
(C) Plastic makes electricity flow faster\
\
(D) Plastic cools down the copper wire\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Plastic is an insulator that protects us from electric shocks.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Plastic does not conduct electricity and safely shields the bare conductive metal wires.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 31. A magnet always has how many poles?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) One\
\
(B) Two (North and South)\
\
(C) Three\
\
(D) Four\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Two (North and South).
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Every magnet, regardless of size or shape, possesses a North pole and a South pole.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 32. What happens when the North pole of one magnet is brought near the North pole of another?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) They attract each other\
\
(B) They repel (push away from) each other\
\
(C) Nothing happens\
\
(D) They melt together\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) They repel (push away from) each other.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Like magnetic poles repel, while opposite poles (North and South) attract.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 33. Which of the following is an example of a reversible change?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Burning of a paper sheet\
\
(B) Melting of ice into water\
\
(C) Baking a raw cake\
\
(D) Cooking an egg\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Melting of ice into water.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Water can be refrozen back into ice by cooling; the change can be easily reversed.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 34. Handpicking is the best method to separate:
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Salt dissolved in clear water\
\
(B) Small pebbles from a bowl of rice grains\
\
(C) Oil mixed with water\
\
(D) Fine tea dust from water\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Small pebbles from a bowl of rice grains.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Handpicking works when impurities are visible, distinct in shape/size, and present in small amounts.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 35. Farmers separate grain seeds from dry harvested stalks using which process?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Winnowing\
\
(B) Threshing\
\
(C) Sieving\
\
(D) Filtration\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Threshing.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Beating stalks on a hard surface or using machines to loosen seeds is threshing.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 36. Common salt is obtained from shallow seawater pools mainly by:
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Condensation\
\
(B) Evaporation\
\
(C) Sieving\
\
(D) Decantation\
\

\f0\b Answer: (B) Evaporation.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  The sun's heat evaporates the water liquid, leaving solid crystals of salt behind.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 37. When no more sugar can dissolve in a glass of water at room temperature, the solution is:
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Saturated\
\
(B) Unsaturated\
\
(C) Insoluble\
\
(D) Dilute\
\

\f0\b Answer: (A) Saturated.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  A saturated solution has dissolved the maximum solute possible at that specific temperature.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 38. Which gas is present in the largest quantity in Earth's atmosphere?
\f2\b0 \
\
(A) Oxygen\
\
(B) Carbon dioxide\
\
(C) Nitrogen\
\
(D) Water vapor\
\

\f0\b Answer: (C) Nitrogen.
\f2\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f2\i0  Nitrogen makes up about 78% of the air by volume.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 39. Which gas in the air is essential for burning (combustion) and animal breathing?
\f2\b0 \
